{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["And he has not washed his hands in water — the zav did not immerse. Thus the Sages interpreted. And it appears to me according to its plain sense: since it stated above: רר בשרו את זובו, or החתים בשרו מזובו — that it dried and adhered to his flesh — therefore it spoke in clean language: and he has not washed his hands in water — that if he did not scrub his flesh thoroughly, and also the orifice of the member that became sealed, as explained in tractate Niddah, even though he immersed, he is still impure, since he did not scrub thoroughly before immersion, and whoever touches him becomes impure. And since the discharge issues from the orifice of the member and he needs to scrub, it used the expression of washing his hands by way of analogy, like what is written about the harlot: אכלה ומחתה פיה ואמרה לא פעלתי און. And likewise: אך מסיך הוא את רגליו — since it speaks about an emission, so too here, washing of hands is about the place of the discharge's emergence from that place. And also the expression שטף proves that it is to remove the filth of something from its place. As in: ומורק ושוטף במים — to remove the fat of a hattat that is in a vessel. And likewise: מים כבירים שוטפים. And likewise: תשטף ספיחיה עפר ארץ. So too here, to remove the discharge that is on his flesh."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Rashbam cites רר בשרו את זובו as if from Scripture; the phrase does not appear verbatim in Leviticus 15 and may reflect his paraphrase of the lemma רא בשרו את זובו in v. 3 or a variant reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:48:55.170221683Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}